WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.890
Sell smarter together: timing, groups and written agreements.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.790
This is the video for module three of Agribusiness and Market Access.

3
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:22.090
Just after harvest, almost everyone is selling, so prices for maize, beans and other storable crops tend to be at their lowest. Selling then gives you cash with no storage costs.

4
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:34.900
Months later, in the lean season, prices tend to rise. But the rise is never guaranteed. Storage has real costs and risks, so it is a business decision, not free money.

5
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.110
And a neighbour saying maize will double is a rumour, not evidence.

6
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.750
Here is how to decide. These numbers are illustrative.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.680
Selling now at 55,000 naira a bag brings 1,100,000 naira.

8
00:00:51.930 --> 00:01:02.640
To store, she needs hermetic bags for about 50,000 naira. And she must borrow for school fees at 5% a month, which adds 60,000 naira of interest.

9
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:17.610
Even good storage loses about 2%, leaving 19.6 bags. Add it up and divide. Storage only pays if the price later is clearly above about 61,700 naira a bag.

10
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:26.020
So Fatima sells 8 bags now to pay the fees without a loan, and stores 12. That removes the interest and halves her risk.

11
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:33.500
Poor grain should be sold, not stored. Dry maize to about 13% moisture or less first.

12
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:40.980
Keep bags on wooden pallets, away from walls. Squeeze out the air and tie each inner layer separately.

13
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:49.520
Protect them from rats, open them as little as possible, and check every few weeks for insects, damp or bad smells.

14
00:01:50.770 --> 00:02:03.060
Many mills and wholesalers will not buy five or ten bags. They want a full truck. These twelve women have 120 bags together, and a feed mill buys at least 100 per delivery.

15
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.880
At the farm gate they would get 55,000 naira a bag.

16
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:19.730
The mill pays 66,000. After truck hire of 3,000 a bag and 500 for loading and records, they keep 62,500 naira.

17
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:31.390
That is 75,000 naira more for each woman, and 900,000 naira for the group. It only works because the group checks every member's bags before loading.

18
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:40.960
Put the deal in writing. Name the crop, grade and specification, such as maximum moisture, and the quantity and delivery dates.

19
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:49.530
Fix the price per kilogram or bag, or a clear formula. Agree where the weighing happens, and that a group member watches it.

20
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:57.200
Write how and when payment comes, what happens to rejected bags, and who you go to first if you disagree.

21
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:07.040
Before any negotiation, know your walk-away option, what you will do if this deal fails. If the offer is below it, say no politely.

22
00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:22.720
Be suspicious of any buyer who asks for a registration or processing fee, promises prices far above the market, or will only talk on WhatsApp. Check the company on the CAC public search and ask other farmers.

23
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:31.750
Pay each woman directly into her own account with a written statement. And never release produce until payment is confirmed in your own account.

24
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:47.460
To remember. Lean-season prices are never guaranteed. Store only if the likely price is well above your break-even. Bulk with other women to reach bigger buyers. Put every deal in writing. And never pay a fee to sell.

25
00:03:48.710 --> 00:04:03.330
Now practise. Calculate your storage break-even. Draft a group selling plan with a one-page agreement. Start your Market Access Project file with your before and after prices, and write a 90-day plan with dated actions.

26
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.620
Continue with the lessons below.
